# ===== CHAPTER 7: Every Square, in Full
# ============================== YEAR 13 ==============================
R(13, "The seed rots", "dribble2")
act("Silke, the Scholar, turns the I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest, and the population holds at x3, 300 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Silke", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. Every province collects a bad harvest this year.\" Population holds at three hundred million, times three, so read the x3 row of your tables and the bad-harvest column. No personal disasters on this card. Rich, a lean year makes a Land Deed worth more to the man selling it than to the one keeping it in a cupboard. Let's have that polite conversation before the auction.",
 "Silke: A bad harvest hurts the paupers most, and Rich is down to twenty-five grain, so a Deed is about to become the most sellable thing he owns. I have a hundred and two, I need twenty for the claim, and I'm watching Noah's purse more closely than my own.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out a bad harvest at x3. Last year's new land pays for the first time: Reina +10, Graham +50, Rich +10, Clark +90, Noah +70, Paddy +30. Silke's income is -10 and Rob's -40, paid into the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "The harvest, friends, every grain counted in front of witnesses: Clark ninety, Noah seventy, Paddy thirty, Graham fifty, our gracious Empress ten, and ten for your humble landlord. Silke and Rob, ten and forty into the Pile, if you please, and thank you. Rent's five, same as it was when I first held this purse, because a lean year is no time to squeeze the people who grow the rice. And Silke, I'd love to have that conversation. You're quite right that a lean year makes the Deed worth more to the man selling it, and that's exactly why the price isn't going down.",
 "Rich: Fifteen in rent takes me to fifty and a half, and that's still thirty grain short of looking like a threat. The Deeds are the only thing I've got that Silke can't buy anywhere else, so I'll make her pay for the polite conversation she's asking for.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich keeps the rent at 5, and each peasant pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "A rotten harvest is no year to squeeze anyone, so it's twenty percent, the lowest the law allows, and Graham, the crown is building your irrigation out of its own purse, all thirty of it. And every new square anyone buys this year, the treasury pays in full. Nobody at this table will have a revolt clause to wave at me, darling. Not one.",
 TH(("Reina", "Gentle tax, the last work owed paid off and every field subsidised means no peasant has a single §16 grievance to sign, and that leaves me free to watch the auction hall. Silke needs one Deed and Rich sells them, so that's the cart I'm watching this spring."),
    ("", "The Province 2 Irrigation System is the last work owed. Once it is built the game ends in the public works step, before any auction (§18).")))
say("Graham", "The tax is collected. Clark eighteen, Noah fourteen, Paddy six, Rich two, and ten from me, fifty in all. I'll count it again in front of you before it goes to the Empress, and Rob, I've written down that you refused. Majesty, I accept the irrigation at thirty, and I'll take all six squares you're offering in full: E11 to E15 and F10. That's a hundred and twenty out of the crown's purse. You said \"every square, in full,\" and I've written it down in those exact words.",
 "Graham: She's paying the whole bill so nobody can wave a revolt clause at her. That means six squares of free land for me, and they'll still be paying me twenty each long after she's forgotten she bought them. I spent a treasury being generous once, and this year I'll let someone else do it. (From the board: Rob's income was -40 this year, so he owed no tax.)")
taxes(.20, collector="Graham, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
buy_land(2, ["E11","E12","E13","E14","E15","F10"], {1:120}, "New land: Graham buys six squares for Province 2, E11 to E15 and F10, and the crown pays all 120, as Reina offered.")
say("Reina", "Graham darling, \"every square, in full\" is in the minutes and the crown pays it, all hundred and twenty, without a murmur. I'll keep my own fields as they are, though. An Empress who's just paid off the last of the realm's works doesn't need to dig up the north-east to prove anything.",
 "Reina: The irrigation was the last work owed, so the game ends the moment it's built: no auction, no march, and the throne stays where it is, with me on it. I watched every cart for twelve years, and in the end I closed the auction hall before anyone could go shopping.")
say("Rich", "Majesty, I've never heard a more generous sentence from a throne, and I sat on one for six years. 'Every new square anyone buys this year, the treasury pays in full.' I'll take the only square I'm offered, F9, and the crown pays all twenty. Graham's written your words down, and I'm glad to be in his minutes for once.",
 "Rich: Twenty grain a year from next harvest for nothing, and I'm only asking the crown for what she promised in public. If she'd had the sense to make her promise narrower, I'd have had to dip into my own forty-eight and a half.")
buy_land(3, ["F9"], {1:20}, "New land: Rich buys F9 for Province 3, and the crown pays all 20.")
say("Silke", "Majesty, I'm holding you to it as well: \"every new square, in full.\" Three squares on my coast, I25, I26 and J26, sixty grain from the treasury, and Graham can put me in the minutes next to Rich. It's a lean year, so a scholar takes what's offered.",
 "Silke: The Storyteller just pointed out what nobody else seems to have noticed. The irrigation is the last work owed, so when it goes up the game ends and there's no auction. My Deed has to come from Rich right now, in a private trade, or not at all.")
buy_land(4, ["I25","I26","J26"], {1:60}, "New land: Silke buys three squares of sea for Province 4, I25, I26 and J26, and the crown pays all 60.")
say("Clark", "Majesty, \"every new square anyone buys this year, the treasury pays in full,\" and Graham's already got it written down word for word, so I'll take the whole ring: J27 to L27, M27 and M28, N28, O28, P28, and Q26 to Q28. Eleven squares, two hundred and twenty from the crown. And for the minutes, that's the first entry I've ever filed under \"Reina, generous\".",
 "Clark: Free land at twenty a square is the cheapest leverage I'll ever be offered, and if the x6 card turns up I'll be the peasant with a coast that still pays. Reina will feel two hundred and twenty leaving her purse, and I'd like to see who she leans on to fill it again.")
buy_land(5, ["J27","K27","L27","M27","M28","N28","O28","P28","Q26","Q27","Q28"], {1:220}, "New land: Clark buys eleven squares of sea for Province 5, J27, K27, L27, M27, M28, N28, O28, P28 and Q26 to Q28, and the crown pays all 220.")
say("Noah", "Majesty, I'll be honest about it, since someone ought to be: Clark's ring has left the treasury on a hundred and four, and five squares is a hundred. So I'll take five, U24 to U26, T27 and S27, all at the crown's cost, 'every square, in full', and that leaves you four grain and your good name. I'm not asking for a sixth. I can count, and the purse can't pay for it.",
 "Noah: That's a hundred more harvest every year from next spring, and my purse stays at a hundred and forty-five and a half for the Deed and the Jar. The far corner is getting crowded with my things, which is just how I like it, because no boot comes down that far.")
buy_land(6, ["U24","U25","U26","T27","S27"], {1:100}, "New land: Noah buys five squares for Province 6, U24 to U26, T27 and S27, and the crown pays all 100. The crown's reserve is down to 4, and the Irrigation System it has promised costs 30.")
say("Paddy", "Majesty, I'd hold you to it too if there was anything left to hold, but I can count as well as Noah and four grain won't dig a ditch. I'll keep my own purse shut and keep my fields as they are. Graham, put me down in the minutes as the one who didn't ask.",
 "Paddy: My four squares from last year already have the farm out of the red, and I've fifty-two and a half grain to spend on a Customs House, a Deed and a Jar, so every grain I sink into mud today is a grain I won't have at the market. And Reina owes the realm thirty for Graham's irrigation with four in the purse, so it does no harm to be the one man she remembers was kind to her.")
say("Silke", "Well. The treasury's down to four grain and a promise, so I'll keep my own purse shut on land, thank you, Majesty. Rob, I've paid you for the view every year since Year 2. I'd like to buy something from you for once.",
 TH(("Silke", "Rich isn't allowed to sell me his Deed, but Rob can sell me the one he stole, and I've offered everything except the twenty I need to make the claim. If Rob says yes before the works step runs, I show five pieces and that chair is mine."),
    ("", "Silke had whispered Rich 65 for a Deed from his stock; unsold stock is sold only at auction (Section 10), so he couldn't take it. The one Land Deed on the board is Rob's raided one on E8.")))
say("Rob", "Silke, my dear, I've had a few kind offers in my time, and that's among the kindest. But nothing on my mantel is for sale. The desert doesn't sell, it collects. And from where I'm standing, your front garden looks well stocked already.",
 "Rob: That Deed is one of my crossed-off names. It's also the very last piece Silke needs to finish her set and take the chair, so seventy-two grain would pay me to lose two games at once. W3 and W5 are a step from her pots, so she can keep her grain, and I'll come round and pick up what I'm owed. (Silke had whispered him 72 for the raided Deed on E8, the one Land Deed on the board.)")
say("Reina", "Well, darling, I said every square in full and I meant it, and you've all held me to it beautifully: four hundred grain of new fields, every one in somebody else's province. Graham, the irrigation is in your fields, the crown has just put a hundred and twenty into your new land, and the crown has four grain left. Put in twenty-six and you'll have a ditch out of it, plus the honour of signing the last entry in your own minutes.",
 "Reina: I wrote a blank cheque and this table cashed it to the last grain, so a woman who plans years ahead just got planned around. If Graham chips in, the last work gets built and the game ends with me on the throne; if he doesn't, I'm an Empress with four grain and sixteen in salaries to find. (From the board: the crown paid 520 for new land this year, not four hundred.)")
say("Graham", "Majesty, the minutes say \"the crown paying all thirty,\" and they're your words, not mine. I won't pay twenty-six to close the book on a page where I hold nothing. The ditch can wait until the treasury can pay for it. Rob refused his tax and I've recorded that, so I'll record that the crown's ditch is still owed as well.",
 "Graham: If that ditch gets dug, the game ends tonight and I finish with an empty shelf and ninety-eight grain, so I'm not buying the shovel. She spent her whole promise on Clark's sea, and her last work can wait for her.")
act("Public works: nothing is built in Year 13. The crown has 4 and the Province 2 Irrigation System costs 30, and Graham won't pay toward it. 30 of the works are still owed, so the game goes on.", map_hl=[1,2], big=True)
say("Graham", "I'm starting from zero again, so the figures are small and honest, one for each piece. Silke, you need one Deed, and everyone here can count to one. I'm not going to make it cheap for you.",
 "Graham: Silke is a Deed away from a full set, so I'll go to fifty-five to keep it from her, and past that it's her chair to take. Everywhere else I bid what a piece is worth to a man with an empty shelf, and I don't go a grain higher.")
say("Rich", "Friends, a landlord with forty-eight grain can't start a war, so I'll be bidding a grain over and never two, as usual. The Deed opens at sixty-five. A lean year doesn't lower the price, and I've heard there's at least one scholar at this table who agrees with me. If she meant it, she can say it again in the auction where everyone can hear.",
 "Rich: Silke offered me sixty-five in a whisper, so now she can pay sixty-five in public, and I'll get my quarter however the chairs go round afterwards. Small bids on the pieces I need, because a cheap Brush or Vase under J18 is the slow way back, and slow is the only way I've ever got there.")
say("Silke", "You all know what I'm after, so I won't pretend otherwise: one Deed, and everything else can go where it likes. Noah, you'd need the Jar as well as the Deed, and Rich has two more Deeds for next year. Stand aside on this one, and when I'm in the chair your fields get the first works the crown builds. Graham can write that down too.",
 "Silke: I can go to seventy-two and still keep the twenty the claim costs. Noah's purse goes deeper than mine, so the only way I win is if he decides he doesn't need to fight me this year.")
say("Clark", "For the minutes: I'm in for the Customs House, the Deed and the Bowl, and out of the Brush and the Vase, so Graham and Rich can sort those out between them. Silke, the Deed's your last piece and I know it, so I'm not starting a war over it. My Jar opens at ten, and anyone who wants it pays for the privilege, same as I did.",
 "Clark: Silke will spend whatever it takes on that Deed, so I'm putting my grain on the Customs House and the Bowl, the only ones in the cart. Rich has three Deeds to sell, so I'll get my turn at one, and leverage means waiting for that turn instead of losing ninety grain chasing it.")
say("Noah", "Here's the sum out loud, since that's my job at this table. I need the Deed and the Jar and twenty to sit down with. Silke needs only the Deed, and on ninety-two and a half she can write seventy-two and still keep her twenty. So I'm writing more than that for Rich's Deed, and whatever's left goes on Clark's Jar. Rich, I'd sooner settle it fair than feed the Pile, but I won't stand aside, not on this one. And my Bowl goes up at five. Somebody ought to own it.",
 "Noah: Seventy-four is a grain and a half over the most Silke can pay and still keep her twenty, and that leaves me fifty-one for the Jar and twenty in the purse, so every grain I have is already spoken for. If Graham or Rich outbids me on the Jar, at least my chair isn't the one worth stealing this year.")
say("Paddy", "I've fifty-two and a half grain and three holes in my set, so I'll not pretend I'm the big money today. But I've come second by one grain too many times, so I'll be in on the Customs House, the Deed and the Jar for as long as my purse lets me. And my Vase is up at two, for anyone who wants a good pot cheap.",
 "Paddy: I can't finish the set this year, so the plan is to pick up one or two pieces cheap while the rich ones fight over the Deed, and leave enough in the purse that I'm not sitting in a debtor's chair. Silke only needs a Deed, so I'll be watching that lot hardest, even if I can't outbid her.")
say("Rob", "Thirteen years and not a single lot, friends, and I see no reason to break the habit now. Bid away. The desert's just here for the show.",
 "Rob: One Land Deed is all Silke needs to finish her set, and she has ninety-two and a half grain, so if she goes for Rich's Deed I'll top her at ninety-five and park the thing in the sand. I'd rather pour grain into the Pile than watch her climb into a chair before my lads get round to her garden.")
act("Minimums: Graham's Customs House 2, Rich's Land Deed Scroll 65, Silke's Bamboo Brush 10, Clark's Dragon Jar 10, Noah's Wooden Bowl 5, Paddy's Vase 2. Rob passes on every lot.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7,8])
DR13 = "Take it with my blessing, and I'll remember what you paid."
DP13 = "That's past what a wheat farmer can carry. Go on, it's yours."
DS13 = "Go on, then. The thread comes round again, it always does."
DG13 = "That's past my figure. Take it, and I've written down what you paid."
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,9), B(3,None,DR13), B(7,18), B(7,None,DP13), B(5,19)],
 hover="Graham's minimum was 2. Clark's limit was 36. Clark puts the Customs House on P25.", at="P25")
lot(3, "LD", [B(4,72), B(4,None,DS13), B(6,73)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 65. Silke's limit was 72, every grain she could spend and keep 20 for a set; Noah's was 74. Graham's 25 (55 to keep it from Silke), Clark's 22 and Paddy's 15 were under the minimum, and Rob would have gone to 95 only to keep it from Silke. Noah puts the Deed on Q19.", at="Q19")
lot(4, "BB", [B(2,10)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 10. Graham's limit was 18; Rich's 8 was under the minimum. Graham puts the Brush on G12.", at="G12")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,16), B(2,None,DG13), B(7,16), B(7,None,DP13), B(6,17)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 10. Noah's limit was 51; Graham's and Paddy's were 16, and Rich's 6 was under the minimum. Noah puts the Jar on Q20.", at="Q20")
act("With the Deed and the Jar, Noah holds the Rice Peasant's full set: a Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Vase, Land Deed Scroll and Dragon Jar, with 55.5 grain. It is complete after the fourth lot, and neither later lot completes anyone's set.", map_hl=[6], big=True)
say("Noah", "Silke, I'm sorry, and I mean that. You offered me the first works if I stood aside, and I told you plainly I wouldn't. Here's the sum, out loud like always: fifty-five and a half of mine plus your ninety-five is a hundred and fifty and a half, and the Scholar's chair. I read the cards for this table in Year One, and I'll read them honestly again. Clark, you need only a Deed now and Rich has two left, so I know whose chair I'm sitting in, and I'll be watching you.",
 "Noah: Taking Silke's seat sends her set back to stock, so it gets rid of one of the two players who were a single Deed from a chair, and it's the biggest purse I'm allowed to take. Four times I've been caught on a full purse with nothing in hand. This time I've done the sum for my own chair first, and the only man who can take it is Clark, and only if he beats the table to one of Rich's Deeds.")
swap(6, 4, "Noah Tall shows the Rice Peasant's complete set (Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Vase, Land Deed Scroll, Dragon Jar) and 55.5 grain, and replaces Silke Weber as the Scholar. He takes her 95. His Bamboo Brush, Province 4's own realia, joins Province 4's unsold stock; his Customs House, Vase, Deed and Jar go to J22, K22, M22 and M23. Silke's Customs House, Jar, Bowl and Vase go back to stock; she draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Rice Peasant, in Province 6, from now on.",
 hover="Section 11: a peasant may replace the Scholar. The Deed Silke needed went to the man who took her seat. Noah named no squares in Province 4, so his pieces go where no soldier can reach this year, as he has always placed them. The last two lots are settled from the same limits.",
 at={"CH":"J22", "VA":"K22", "LD":"M22", "DJ":"M23"})
lot(6, "WB", [B(2,18), B(2,None,DG13), B(5,19)],
 hover="Noah's minimum was 5; the Bowl is now Silke's to sell, as the Rice Peasant, and she keeps the quarter. Clark's limit was 30. Clark puts the Bowl on O26.", at="O26")
lot(7, "VA", [B(3,8), B(3,None,DR13), B(2,9)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Graham's limit was 18. Graham puts the Vase on G13.", at="G13")
say("Clark", "Customs House on P25 and the Bowl on O26, both down on the coast beside the Brush and well out of any soldier's walk. Paddy, out at eighteen this time, and it's my turn to say \"by one\", but I won't write it on your page. Noah, you're right to watch me. Rich has two Deeds left, and I'll be on the far side of the table when they come up.",
 "Clark: That's four of five, all on the far south-east corner where the Warlord needs a week to reach, and I've got about sixty grain left for next year's Deed. Silke's just lost her chair and her set, so the hungriest bidder for that Deed is out of the race, and I'm the man waiting on the last lot again, only this time I've read my own diary.")
say("Graham", "A Brush for ten and a Vase for nine. That's nineteen grain for two pieces, after years of paying fifty for one. They'll go at the back of the G column, where nobody's boots reach. Silke, I'm sorry. I said I wouldn't make the Deed cheap for you, and I didn't need to, because Noah made it dear.",
 "Graham: Two pieces cheap while everyone else threw seventy at the Deed. Clark needs only a Deed now and Rich has two more, so next year the man to watch is in the silk fields, not on the throne.")
march({"W1":"I12", "W2":"J15", "W3":"I19", "W4":"L25", "W5":"L19"}, take={"W4":("VA","M18")}, text="Robert Land marches: W1 onto I12 in Province 2 (Graham pays 5); W2 to J15; W3 onto I19 in Province 1 (Reina owes 10, with 4 in the reserve); W4 onto L25 in Province 5, taking Clark's Vase to M18 on the desert; W5 onto L19 in Province 4 (Noah, the new Scholar, pays 5).",
 hover="Rob: The Vase doesn't count for me, but Clark needed one Deed to take a chair, and now he needs a Vase as well, so another set is off the table. Meanwhile W1 is heading for Graham's pots on the G column, and W2, W3 and W5 are all closing on Rich's Bowl on J17, which leaves two names to go and three rounds to get them.")
say("Rob", "Clark, you said the coast was well out of any soldier's walk. W4 has always been a keen walker. I'll look after your Vase on M18, and you can pop by and see it whenever you like. Graham, a little something from your fields as well, and Majesty, my lad's knocking at the north-east door, so do have a look in the cushions.")
salaries({2:8, 4:8}, text="Salaries: Reina pays what she declared, 8 to the Official, Graham, and 8 to the new Scholar, Noah. The crown is already 6 in debt to the Harvest Pile after W3's raid, and has no realia to sell, so the salaries take its debt to 22.",
 hover="Rulebook, Running out of grain: a salary the Emperor owes is a payment like any other; with no realia to sell back, the shortfall is a debt to the Pile. A player in debt can't bid, buy, trade, give grain, fund works or change seats, and every bag received pays the debt first. Any player in debt is a cause for revolt (Section 16).")
